ure/14037232/state.json" "file_sha256" "49b31ad1a05457584201387d19f74db6b0d744fd65410b817af4d8556f116c56" "basis" 1257 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-9412" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the expression $f(x,y) = (3-x)(y+1)^2.$  What are the missing entries in the sign table of $f(x,y)$ shown below?\n\n| $y > -1$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y <               -1$ |  |  |\n|  | $x<   3$ | $x> 3$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/example-9412.json" "file_sha256" "d63aa26939e8281fbf37fe33b7b5a076022312b553f0a4a9cd7ede8afa6241be" "basis" 1257 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-9412" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x = 3$ and $y = -1.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/df8519853487a7da738b4b3c477b141e00287c67dcb9eb804cbd385c6f43b611.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/4666739c474f85cbf8de7e7ca183206ab7b0bd804816a5504f9c1d97b03956b4.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(3-x)$ | $(y+1)^2$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-left | $(2,0)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(4,0)$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(2,-2)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(4,-2)$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n\n \n\nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/e789ce41c696b456e0c9d79ba8ce60aba030e454ab614902ea13c8d9ccd02d8c.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n| $y > -1$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y <               -1$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n|  | $x<  3$ | $x> 3$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/example-9412.json" "file_sha256" "d63aa26939e8281fbf37fe33b7b5a076022312b553f0a4a9cd7ede8afa6241be" "basis" 1257 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-9414" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $f(x,y) = (x-2)^2(y+1).$ Which of the following shaded areas gives the solution to the inequality $f(x,y) > 0?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/example-9414.json" "file_sha256" "1a04175dc6f51c032ea286c75efeea59a25cdd7a3c8b2bfc1be10dd55e0bc1d4" "basis" 1257 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-9414" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=2$ and $y=-1.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below. Since we have a strict inequality, we use dashed lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/8e635d2ba2e7ca5721ff8b1dec2055b4ac0c9bcc2eca648848c27190271e92b6.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/1ce934096086de33423910710a010400922ea86c66bbfe0b1bfb0d92291ab59e.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(x-2)^2$ | $(y+1)$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(1,0)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(3,0)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{blue}+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(1,-2)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(3,-2)$ | $\\color{blue}+$ | $\\color{red}-$ | $\\color{red}-$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y) > 0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/assets/70a2ca01e47e91f9d818599886f79b0f00edad0cfd12b080995dfbe6cdf68235.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/example-9414.json" "file_sha256" "1a04175dc6f51c032ea286c75efeea59a25cdd7a3c8b2bfc1be10dd55e0bc1d4" "basis" 1257 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14037232/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-121315" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x,y)=-y(x-2).$ What are the missing entries in the following sign table of $f(x,y)?$\n\n| $y>0$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ |  |  |\n|  | $x<2$ | $x>2$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/state.json" "file_sha256" "ff63683317b1ff68128f81c27594160ddb5912a52e3945dd03e8f649d0905b33"} {"question" "q-121315" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=2$ and $y=0.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/41e876c3444c2fc9a5103c16b9c6c3b34a4c7596082e4c8235a5e86cd57f8570.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/c21e4edde44891c4173420575268750fc0f1f407ba8503953de86a9cc72297dc.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $-y$ | $(x-2)$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(1,1)$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| Top-Right | $(3,1)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(1,-1)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(3,-1)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\n \n\nUsing the *last* column of the table above, we get the following picture:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/b668aa9647600567c5d6deccbe3830a26561c06f920e2db2364e632f26c39eff.png)\n\nFinally, we fill in the required sign table.\n\n| $y>0$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n|  | $x<2$ | $x>2$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/state.json" "file_sha256" "ff63683317b1ff68128f81c27594160ddb5912a52e3945dd03e8f649d0905b33"} {"question" "q-121315" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b8535935689f512014d69f4f37d9eb2fb99191853e23ef5af94320df3ebeec9a"} {"question" "q-121315" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/state.json" "file_sha256" "ff63683317b1ff68128f81c27594160ddb5912a52e3945dd03e8f649d0905b33"} {"question" "q-121315" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "| $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $-$ |"] ["text" "| $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-$ | $+$ |"] ["text" "| $-$ | $+$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $-$ |"] ["text" "| $-$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $+$ | $+$ |"] ["text" "| $-$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-$ | $-$ |"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/state.json" "file_sha256" "ff63683317b1ff68128f81c27594160ddb5912a52e3945dd03e8f649d0905b33"} {"question" "q-121315" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "| $+$ | $-$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $-$ | $+$ |"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/state.json" "file_sha256" "ff63683317b1ff68128f81c27594160ddb5912a52e3945dd03e8f649d0905b33"} {"question" "q-149548" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x,y)=(1-x)(y+2)^{2}.$ What are the missing entries in the following sign table of $f(x,y)?$\n\n| $y>-2$ |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<-2$ |  |  |\n|  | $x<1$ | $x>1$ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/state.json" "file_sha256" "ff63683317b1ff68128f81c27594160ddb5912a52e3945dd03e8f649d0905b33"} {"question" "q-149548" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that $f(x,y)$ equals zero when $x=1$ and $y=-2.$ These lines divide the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/345665733b65