ity, we use solid lines to divide the plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/83fcb50be8105d19e044245edd1ad492daad4658ffe4a184ce4bd632dd78e4d4.png)\n\nTo determine the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region, we pick four test points, one for each region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/099429ef29700b1eb832aee297b3d237737260a44dd01d6fc516b07710758cce.png)\n\nNext, we calculate the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each region using a sign table:\n\n|  | Test point | $(x-3)$ | $(y+1)^{2}$ | $f(x,y)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Top-Left | $(2,0)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Top-Right | $(4,0)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| Bottom-Left | $(2,-2)$ | $-$ | $+$ | $-$ |\n| Bottom-Right | $(4,-2)$ | $+$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n\n \n\nFinally, on our diagram, we shade in the solutions to $f(x,y)\\ge 0$ using the *last* column of the table above.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/assets/15efb1840d7a41f4600d05dcedc0c2ea5149c85e8bc1b502d62130a7da09d1b8.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/state.json" "file_sha256" "ff63683317b1ff68128f81c27594160ddb5912a52e3945dd03e8f649d0905b33"} {"question" "q-121273" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041262/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b8535935689f512014d69f4f37d9eb2fb99191853e23ef5af94320df3ebeec9a"} {"question" "q-121273" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable